 3 3\n3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3\n3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3\n8 8 8 8 8 8 8 3 8 8 8 8 8 8 8 3 8 3 8 3 3 3 3 3\n8 3 8 3 8 3 8 8 8 8 8 8 8 8 3 8 3 8 3 3 3 3 3 3\n3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3\n3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3\n3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3 3\nWrong attempts used: 1/8. Demo requests used: 2/8.\nReply with exactly one action JSON on the last line.","reply":"","steps":[],"action":{"type":"invalid"}},{"step":95,"kind":"reprompt","phase":"act","t0":1793238493.184,"t1":1793238527.364,"alias":"task-41ad435a","message":"I could not find a valid action in your reply (empty reply). Reply with your program in one ```python block and {\"action\": \"submit\"} on the last line, or with {\"action\": \"request_demos\"}.","reply":"Both demos: the globally majority color changes to 1, but only in rows that are mixed (contain both colors); pure rows are unchanged. My earlier attempts applied it per-row or globally. Fixing to global-majority, mixed-rows-only.\n\n```python\ndef transform(grid):\n    from collections import Counter\n    c = Counter(v for row in grid for v in row)\n    colors = list(c)\n    if len(colors) < 2:\n        return [row[:] for row in grid]\n    majority = max(colors, key=lambda x: c[x])\n    out = []\n    for row in grid:\n        if any(v == majority for v in row) and any(v != majority for v in row):\n            out.append([1 if v == majority else v for v in row])\n        else:\n            out.append(row[:])\n    return out\n```\n\n{\"action\": \"submit\"}","steps":[],"action":{"type":"submit","correct":false,"valid":true}},{"step":95,"kind":"observation","phase":"act","t0":1793238527.554,"t1":1793238542.824,"alias":"task-41ad435a","message":"Feedback since your last action:\nYour submission was INCORRECT. Wrong attempts so far: 2.\n\nSame instance. Task id: task-41ad435a\nTest input (12x24):\n8 8 8 8 3 8 8 8 8 8 8 8 3 8 3 8 3 8 3 8 3 8 3 8\n3 8 3 8 8 8